    {
      "column_index": 2,
      "header_id": "C9",
      "x_range": [
        1137,
        1890.0
      ],
      "section_title": "Lists: Wrapping",
      "section_number": 3,
      "teaching_text": "3- Lists: Wrapping\r\n\r\n- Wrapping means that the List starts again at the end without stopping.\r\n\r\n- The below List has 10 items, first index is 0 and last index is 9.\r\n\r\n- Try to choose the 10th index with the \"List Item\" component.\r\n\r\n- The \"List Item\" component has a BOOLEAN INPUT \"W\".\r\n\r\n- In Grasshopper, BOOLEAN does not mean geometry merge as in other software, it rather means \"true\" or \"false\".\r\n\r\n- In this case, let us see what happens when we choose the item with 10th idex (which does not exist since the last item has the index 9).\r\n\r\n- With a \"True\" BOOLEAN, the List seems to loop and start again from the beginning, hence the 10th item is 1.\r\n\r\n- With a \"False\" BOOLEAN, the List gives a \"null\" result. In other words, it is saying that there is no 10th item to be displayed.",
      "teaching_panel_id": "C303",
      "additional_teaching_panels": [],
      "sub_annotations": [],
      "concepts_taught": [
        "List Item",
        "true",
        "false",
        "null"
      ],
